# A block of one's own with two inputs: the names after define stand for the numbers a call gives,
# in order, first to first. Set in the old walled city: its walls to climb, its temple garden and
# its market.
lesson coding-a-block-with-two-inputs v=1 format=teach grade=5 unit=7 subject=coding levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "A block of your own with two inputs"
  goal "Make and use a block that takes two numbers, giving each number to its own name in order."

  look {
    scene 36x17 {
      program p code=["define box width height", "  repeat 2", "    forward width", "    turn left", "    forward height", "    turn left", "box 4 2"] at=canvas(1, 0)
      turtle t cols=6 rows=4 col=1 row=4 face=right moves=["repeat 2", "  forward 4", "  turn left", "  forward 2", "  turn left"] right-of=p gap=1
    }
    say """
      In the old walled city the turtle marks out gardens of every size with one block of its own.
      The block has two inputs, width and height. When the program says box 4 2, the first number
      goes to the first name, so width is 4, and the second goes to the second, so height is 2. Box
      2 4 would draw a garden that is tall instead of wide.
      """
  }
  do {
    show coding.inputs-jump a=2 b=1 c=1 d=2 easy-a=1 hard-a=3 hard-c=3
    show coding.inputs-box a=4 b=2 easy-a=3 hard-a=5 hard-b=3
    level easy {
      worked coding.inputs-buy a=2 p=3 c=1 q=2 level=medium
    }
    show coding.inputs-fill w=4 h=2 f=0 easy-w=3 hard-f=1 hard-h=1
    show coding.inputs-buy a=2 p=4 c=3 q=1 easy-a=1 easy-c=2 hard-a=3 hard-c=4
    show coding.inputs-missing a=2 p=3 c=3 q=4 easy-c=2 hard-c=4 hard-q=5
    show coding.inputs-build k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show coding.inputs-fill w=3 h=4 f=1 easy-f=0 easy-h=1 hard-w=4 hard-h=2
    show coding.inputs-missing a=1 p=4 c=2 q=3 easy-p=3 easy-q=2 hard-c=3 hard-q=6
    level hard {
      show coding.inputs-box a=6 b=4
    }
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show coding.inputs-frame v=1 k=1 easy-v=0 hard-v=2
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show coding.inputs-fewest k=0 easy-k=1 hard-k=2
  }
  remember {
    say "A block's inputs are filled in order: the first number goes to the first name after define, the second to the second."
  }

  grown-ups """
    One block with inputs does the work of many blocks with fixed numbers, and the order of the
    inputs is what makes it work. The wrong ideas to watch for are swapping the two numbers, and
    reading a name such as width as a number that never changes. In the child's working, look for
    the block written out again for one call with the numbers put in place of the names. If the
    child is stuck, write the two names on two cards and lay the call's numbers on them, first card
    first; stay another day if the numbers keep landing on the wrong names. Off the page, write a
    recipe step such as "stir for minutes at speed" and fill it in two different ways. The two-star
    question adds 1 inside the block and swaps the inputs in the choices, and the three-star
    question has no method on the page: every climb must go up at least one square. The build
    question has the child write the block and call it twice; each call's second number is fixed on
    its card, so the order of the calls matters as much as the numbers.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the climbs are short and the first input is 1 or 2, one shopping program is worked in the teacher's pen, the gardens are small, the numbers to find for box are the frame's own sides, the market buys are two or fewer of a thing, the missing price is for two things, the frame to call is the smallest, and the fewest climbs is to a flag the same distance across as up. The two climbs to build are 2 across and 2 up, then 1 across and 3 up."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the climbs go three squares across, the gardens are bigger, one call to find uses a block that adds 1 to its width, so the call is one less than the side, the buys are three or four of a thing, the missing price is shared among three or four things, the frame to call is bigger, and the fewest climbs is to a flag nine across and four up, where the climbs across set the count. The two climbs to build are 3 across and 3 up, then 2 across and 2 up, with more towers in the way."
  }
}
